---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CommercialContinuousHjcSasModeler from '../../components/CommercialContinuousHjcSasModeler.astro';

const pageTitle = "Commercial Continuous Hydrodynamic Jet Cavitation-Assisted Supercritical Antisolvent Micronization (HJC-SAS) Modeler | IsItKitchenSafe";
const pageDescription = "Simulate continuous high-velocity liquid micro-jet cavitation impingement inside high-pressure dense-phase supercritical CO2 antisolvent (120–200 bar, 35–42°C, v_jet > 100 m/s) across opposed ruby/sapphire micro-orifices for instantaneous micro-mixing (Da < 0.05, tau_mix < 0.8 ms), extreme supersaturation ratios (S > 1000), and uniform sub-80 nm amorphous polyphenol co-crystal precipitation with in-line residual solvent stripping (<50 ppm) under FDA 21 CFR § 117 and USP <467>.";

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Tools', href: '/tools/' },
  { label: 'Commercial Continuous HJC-SAS Modeler' }
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How does opposed hydrodynamic jet cavitation achieve instantaneous micro-mixing (Da < 0.05, tau_mix < 0.8 ms) in supercritical antisolvent precipitation?",
    answer: "In conventional supercritical antisolvent (SAS) processes, an organic solution is injected through a single capillary or atomization nozzle into supercritical CO2. However, characteristic mixing time (tau_mix) is constrained by macro-turbulent diffusion and often exceeds 15–50 ms. When the Damköhler number Da = tau_mix / tau_ind exceeds 1.0, nucleation occurs in a non-uniform concentration gradient, causing broad particle size distributions, agglomeration, and premature recrystallization. In the HJC-SAS process, two opposing high-velocity micro-jets (v_jet > 100–140 m/s) collide inside a high-pressure supercritical CO2 chamber. The collision unsteadiness and violent Rayleigh-Plesset cavitation bubble collapse generate ultra-fine Kolmogorov micro-eddies (<8 µm), compressing the characteristic micro-mixing time tau_mix below 0.8 ms. With Da < 0.05, micro-mixing is orders of magnitude faster than nucleation induction, producing spatial homogeneity throughout the collision volume."
  },
  {
    question: "Why does HJC-SAS generate amorphous solid dispersions (ASDs) and co-crystals rather than crystalline macro-particles?",
    answer: "Amorphous solid dispersions offer maximum aqueous solubility because molecules are trapped in a disordered, high-energy thermodynamic state without a crystal lattice barrier. When opposing micro-jets collide in dense-phase supercritical CO2 (120–200 bar, 35–42°C), instantaneous mass transfer between the organic solvent (e.g., ethanol) and scCO2 causes instantaneous desolvation. The supersaturation ratio S = C / C_sat explodes beyond 1000 within less than one millisecond. According to classical nucleation theory, homogenous nucleation rate J_hom scales exponentially with supersaturation (J_hom ∝ exp(-B / (ln S)²)). Millions of critical nuclei precipitate simultaneously before solute molecules can orient into ordered crystal lattices. Co-dissolved hydrophilic polymers (such as PVP-K30, cyclodextrin, or phospholipids) form immediate steric barriers around the amorphous nuclei, locking in sub-80 nm particle dimensions."
  },
  {
    question: "How does in-line dynamic supercritical CO2 stripping achieve residual ethanol concentrations below 50 ppm (USP <467>)?",
    answer: "Traditional solvent extraction and spray drying often leave 800–3,500 ppm of residual organic solvent trapped within particle pores, requiring days of secondary vacuum drying that can oxidize delicate polyphenols. In the HJC-SAS skid, high-pressure supercritical CO2 serves a dual function: it acts as the initial antisolvent during impingement and subsequently acts as a dynamic continuous washing solvent as the wet aerosol travels through the collection chamber. Operating at mass ratios R_m between 25:1 and 50:1, scCO2 exhibits infinite miscibility with Class 3 organic solvents like ethanol and ethyl lactate. Continuous scCO2 cross-flow rapidly extracts ethanol from the precipitated nanocrystal cake. When discharged through the dynamic sintered metal filter, residual solvent levels are consistently verified below 50 ppm—vastly outperforming the 5,000 ppm limit established under USP <467> and ICH Q3C guidelines."
  },
  {
    question: "Why does HJC-SAS provide a 14x to 28x enhancement in gastrointestinal dissolution rate and oral bioavailability?",
    answer: "Hydrophobic polyphenols and nutraceuticals (e.g., curcumin, quercetin, resveratrol, CoQ10) belong to BCS Class II (low solubility, high permeability), where dissolution rate in gastric fluid is the strict rate-limiting step for systemic bioavailability. The Noyes-Whitney equation dictates that dissolution rate dM/dt is proportional to surface area A and saturation solubility C_s (dM/dt = (D · A / h) · (C_s - C)). HJC-SAS delivers a two-fold thermodynamic advantage: (1) Micronizing particle diameter from 5,000 nm down to sub-80 nm expands specific surface area A by over 60-fold; and (2) Eliminating the crystal lattice enthalpy of fusion elevates apparent solubility C_s by 4x to 8x due to the amorphous state. In vitro dissolution assays demonstrate greater than 90% bioactive release in gastric media within 5 minutes, compared to less than 6% for raw crystalline powder."
  },
  {
    question: "What sanitary engineering and high-pressure safety standards govern the commercial HJC-SAS skid?",
    answer: "The industrial HJC-SAS commercial skid is engineered to comply with leading pharmaceutical and food-grade hygiene standards: (1) FDA 21 CFR § 117 (FSMA cGMP) for sanitary food manufacturing; (2) USP <467> for Class 3 Residual Solvents and USP <429> for Laser Diffraction Particle Size Analysis; (3) 3-A Sanitary Standards 63-04 (Sanitary Fittings) and 08-17 (Diaphragm Valves) fabricated from electropolished 316L stainless steel with surface roughness Ra < 0.4 µm; (4) EHEDG Type EL Class I Clean-in-Place (CIP) certification validated against alkaline and peracetic acid washes; and (5) ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code Section VIII Division 1 and ASME B31.3 High-Pressure Process Piping designed for continuous operating pressures up to 300 bar with redundant dual-burst rupture discs."
  }
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": faqs.map(faq => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": faq.question,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": faq.answer
    }
  }))
};

const toolSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  "name": "Commercial Continuous Hydrodynamic Jet Cavitation-Assisted Supercritical Antisolvent Micronization (HJC-SAS) Modeler",
  "applicationCategory": "EngineeringApplication",
  "operatingSystem": "All",
  "description": "Simulate continuous high-velocity liquid micro-jet cavitation impingement inside high-pressure dense-phase supercritical CO2 antisolvent (120–200 bar, 35–42°C, v_jet > 100 m/s) across opposed ruby/sapphire micro-orifices for instantaneous micro-mixing (Da < 0.05, tau_mix < 0.8 ms), extreme supersaturation ratios (S > 1000), and uniform sub-80 nm amorphous polyphenol co-crystal precipitation with in-line residual solvent stripping (<50 ppm) under FDA 21 CFR § 117 and USP <467>.",
  "offers": {
    "@type": "Offer",
    "price": "0",
    "priceCurrency": "USD"
  }
};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-8">
    <div class="mb-6">
      <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />
    </div>

    <!-- Modeler Component -->
    <CommercialContinuousHjcSasModeler lang="en" />

    <!-- FAQs Section -->
    <section class="mt-14 bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-10 shadow-sm">
      <div class="mb-8 border-b border-hairline/80 pb-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-tight mb-2">
          Frequently Asked Questions: HJC-SAS Micro-Jet Cavitation & Supercritical Micronization
        </h2>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted">
          Authoritative pharmaceutical engineering, cavitation hydrodynamics, and food-grade regulatory compliance principles for industrial supercritical antisolvent precipitation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        {faqs.map(faq => (
          <div class="border-b border-hairline/60 pb-5 last:border-b-0 last:pb-0">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mb-2">
              {faq.question}
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
              {faq.answer}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Cross-Linking Navigation -->
    <section class="mt-12 p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-between gap-4">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink">Explore Adjacent Supercritical & Nano-Fluidic Diagnostic Tools</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted">Review our suite of high-pressure fluid engineering and aseptic processing modelers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-3">
        <a href="/tools/commercial-continuous-uhps-mfs-modeler/" class="px-4 py-2 rounded-xl bg-surface border border-hairline text-ink hover:text-cyan-400 font-medium text-xs transition-colors">
          UHPS-MFS Modeler &rarr;
        </a>
        <a href="/tools/commercial-continuous-al-sad-aerogel-modeler/" class="px-4 py-2 rounded-xl bg-surface border border-hairline text-ink hover:text-indigo-400 font-medium text-xs transition-colors">
          AL-SAD Aerogel Modeler &rarr;
        </a>
        <a href="/tools/" class="px-4 py-2 rounded-xl bg-indigo-600 hover:bg-indigo-700 text-white font-medium text-xs transition-colors">
          All 172 Tools &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(toolSchema)} />
</Layout>
